course: dictionary
lesson: 611
title: Ax, axe, and one word with two jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can be a thing or an action. «Ax» can name a tool: an ax has a heavy metal blade on a long handle, and it is used for chopping wood. Look at the other words in the sentence to see which job the word is doing.
explain: The same word can also mean to cancel. «The show was axed» means the show was cancelled, not chopped. Try the simple meaning in the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
explain: Spelling changes from place to place. American English writes «ax», while British, Irish, Canadian, Australian and New Zealand English usually write «axe». Both spellings name the same tool, so you can read either one.
explain: Axes are very sharp. Only trained adults should use them, so never touch one without an adult.
fact: «Ax» means a tool with a heavy metal blade on a long handle, used for chopping wood.
fact: In informal English, «to ax» something means to cancel it or cut it; «the show was axed» means it was cancelled.
fact: «Ax» is the American spelling; «axe» is the spelling used in British, Irish, Canadian, Australian and New Zealand English.
fact: Vikings fought with battle-axes, which were axes used in war.
fact: Terror birds had hooked beaks like axes; some were about 3 metres tall, and they lived until about 2 million years ago.
exercise: «Terror birds had hooked beaks like axes.» What is an ax? | A kind of wet, grey weather | *A tool with a heavy metal blade on a long handle | A bird with a sharp, curved beak | A show that has been cancelled
exercise: The news says «The show was axed.» What does axed mean here? | *Cancelled | Made longer | Chopped into pieces with a tool | Painted bright blue
exercise: Which word is the British spelling for the chopping tool? | *axe | axes-hook | ax | cancel
test: «Some terror birds were about 3 metres tall.» About how tall were they? | About 3 kilometres | *About 3 metres | About 3 centimetres | About 30 metres
test: The council decided to ax the Saturday football club. What does ax mean here? | Make the club bigger | Chop down the goal posts | Paint the football pitch | *Cancel the club
exam: Which sentence uses ax to mean a cutting tool? | The show was axed last week. | *The farmer split logs with a sharp ax. | The school axed the trip to the zoo.
